    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start for the intro. Can you add a section that highlights why this concept is so necessary compared to just having procedural due process? I want the kids to really understand why we can't just rely on the government following the right steps if the laws themselves are inherently unjust.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Procedural due process only ensures the *right way of the game* is followed, like making sure you get a fair trial, but it won't stop the government from making a fundamentally biased rule. Substantive due process is necessary because it allows the law to be *fair in the first place*, directly challenging the idea that the government can legally violate fundamental rights, regardless of the correct legal process. In short, one is about the *how* of the law, while the other is about the *right to have the law in the first place to be fair to you and your fundamental rights",
      "source": "target",
